          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get chest discomfort only when I am anxious, never on exertion. How do I tell the difference, and what does guidance describe about assessing it?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now if you have chest discomfort that is new, pressing, or not clearly explained.** Do not drive yourself. The tool flagged this as an emergency because chest discomfort can be a sign of a heart attack, and treatment works best in the first minutes. If you are unsure, treat it as urgent and call.\n\nThe guidance I could find does not give a reliable way to tell anxiety-related chest discomfort from heart-related pain at home:\n\n- The emergency chest pain guidance (red-flag-chest-pain) says any chest discomfort with pressure, tightness, squeezing or heaviness lasting more than a few minutes, spreading to the arm, jaw, neck or back, or with breathlessness, sweating, nausea or light-headedness, is treated as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise.\n- The coronary artery disease note (condition-coronary-artery-disease) says heart pain is often not the classic pattern, especially in women, older people and people with diabetes. Symptoms can be subtle.\n- The heartburn note (condition-gerd) says heartburn and heart pain cannot be reliably told apart by how they feel.\n- The anxiety and activity note (lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity) says anxiety can cause body sensations, such as a fast heartbeat and sweating, that people often read as a sign of something wrong. It does not say anxiety chest discomfort is safe to assume.\n\nThe guidance does not say that discomfort which only happens during anxiety is harmless. It also does not cover how often your symptoms occur or what a clinician would check. Because you have had this discomfort and cannot be sure of its cause, the right next step is to talk with a doctor. Ask them to assess the chest discomfort itself, not just the anxiety. Tell them exactly when it happens, how long it lasts and what it feels like.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
